    "summary": "Buffer overflow with AES key wrap with padding in `cipher_update`",
    "details": "`CipherCtxRef::cipher_update`, `CipherCtxRef::cipher_update_vec` and `symm::Crypter::update` require (or allocate) `input.len() + block_size` bytes of output. With the AES key-wrap-with-padding ciphers (`EVP_aes_128_wrap_pad`, `EVP_aes_192_wrap_pad`, `EVP_aes_256_wrap_pad`), OpenSSL pads the input to a multiple of 8 bytes and adds an 8-byte block, so when the input length is not a multiple of 8 it writes up to 7 bytes past the end of the caller's buffer or `Vec`. If an attacker can influence the plaintext length, this is heap corruption they can partly control.\n\nOnly users of the AES key-wrap-with-padding ciphers are affected. 0.10.79 sizes the output for these ciphers as the input length rounded up to a multiple of 8, plus 8.",
